## Why this matters

The capstone extends the browser-performance case study with a real concurrency decision. A page needs to process a stream of numeric frames without stalling input. Workers with `postMessage` copy each frame; a `SharedArrayBuffer` with `Atomics` lets the main thread and workers coordinate over one buffer without copies. The lab asks you to build both, measure them under load, and write the diagnosis and the rollback so a teammate could reverse the decision.

## The simple version

Think of a shared whiteboard between rooms: everyone can write and read at once, so a protocol decides who writes where and how a reader knows a line is complete. `Atomics.store` and `Atomics.load` are pen strokes that cannot be torn in half; `Atomics.wait` and `Atomics.notify` let a worker sleep on a cell until someone writes it. The analogy breaks at memory ordering: without atomics, one thread can observe writes in a different order than they were made, and a plain read of a "ready" flag can see the flag before the data it guards. The flag itself must be atomic and set last.

## The lab

Shared memory needs cross-origin isolation. Run the setup in an isolated directory; it creates a static page, a worker, and a small dev server that sends the two required headers. Record the browser and version you test with; header names and availability are stated as of 2026.

```bash
lab=$(mktemp -d) && cd "$lab" || exit 1
cat > server.mjs <<'JS'
import { createServer } from 'node:http'; import { readFile } from 'node:fs/promises'; import { extname } from 'node:path';
const types = { '.html': 'text/html', '.js': 'text/javascript', '.mjs': 'text/javascript' };
createServer(async (req, res) => {
  const file = req.url === '/' ? 'index.html' : req.url.slice(1);
  try {
    const body = await readFile(file);
    res.writeHead(200, { 'Content-Type': types[extname(file)] ?? 'application/octet-stream',
      'Cross-Origin-Opener-Policy': 'same-origin', 'Cross-Origin-Embedder-Policy': 'require-corp' });
    res.end(body);
  } catch { res.writeHead(404).end(); }
}).listen(8080, () => console.log('http://localhost:8080'));
JS
printf '<!doctype html><script type="module" src="main.js"></script><pre id="out"></pre>\n' > index.html
printf 'lab at %s\n' "$lab"
```

## Core mechanics

`main.js` creates one `SharedArrayBuffer` holding a ring of frames plus a control `Int32Array` with a write index, a read index and a "frames ready" cell. Workers spin up from `worker.js`, `Atomics.wait` on the ready cell, process the frame at the read index, and `Atomics.add` a completion counter. The main thread writes the frame data first and only then `Atomics.store`s the new write index and `Atomics.notify`s the waiters; the data is published by the atomic write of the index, which is what makes the ordering observable and correct.

The comparison pipeline sends each frame with `postMessage` (optionally transferring the buffer). Both pipelines report frames per second and the main thread's longest task, measured with `performance.now` around each dispatch and with a `PerformanceObserver` for `longtask` entries. `crossOriginIsolated` must be `true` or the shared version cannot run; the page shows which pipeline is active and why.

## Runnable experiment

Predict each line, then run the server with `node server.mjs` and open the page.

```js
// in the page console after loading main.js
console.log(crossOriginIsolated);            // true: both headers were sent
const sab = new SharedArrayBuffer(16); const ctrl = new Int32Array(sab);
Atomics.store(ctrl, 0, 1); console.log(Atomics.load(ctrl, 0)); // 1
console.log(Atomics.notify(ctrl, 0, 1));      // 0: no worker is waiting on this cell yet
```

Then remove the `Cross-Origin-Embedder-Policy` header from the server, reload, and record what `crossOriginIsolated` and `new SharedArrayBuffer(16)` do now.

## Failure modes

Reading the frame data after a plain (non-atomic) flag read can observe stale bytes. `Atomics.wait` is not allowed on the main thread in browsers; only workers may block. Cross-origin isolation blocks embedding of resources that do not opt in with CORP or CORS, so a page that loads third-party images may break; the rollback note must list what stopped loading. A pipeline that wins on throughput but doubles the main thread's longest task has failed the responsiveness constraint.

## Practical exercise

Deliver the case-study extension: both pipelines behind one switch; a measurement table (frames per second, longest task, memory) for a fixed workload on a fixed device; a diagnosis naming which mechanism explains each difference; the ordering argument for the shared version (why the index write publishes the data); and a rollback note listing the isolation headers, what they broke, and the exact steps to return to the `postMessage` pipeline.

exercise.starter_code:
// worker.js (sketch)
let ctrl, frames;
self.onmessage = ({ data }) => {
  ctrl = new Int32Array(data.control); frames = new Float32Array(data.frames);
  for (;;) {
    Atomics.wait(ctrl, 0, 0);            // sleep until a frame index is published
    const index = Atomics.load(ctrl, 0);   // atomic read publishes the frame written before it
    process(frames.subarray(index * 1024, (index + 1) * 1024));
    Atomics.add(ctrl, 1, 1);               // completion counter
    Atomics.store(ctrl, 0, 0);
  }
};
